The current position contains no immediate winning move for o (player to move) and no immediate winning threats that x can convert on the following turn. The only existing three-in-a-row for o is the diagonal (3,2)-(4,3)-(5,4). Its completion square (2,1) is not yet droppable, so the three is not an immediate threat.

The only moves that create an immediate (single) threat are therefore:
- column 1, which fills (3,1) and thereby makes (2,1) droppable;
- column 2, which extends the vertical pair in column 2 into a three whose completion square (1,2) is immediately droppable.

Both threats are answerable by x, and neither answering move itself wins or creates a counter-threat. Column 2 is nevertheless strictly stronger:
- it occupies the intersection of several still-open lines (horizontal on row 2 and the / diagonal of sum 4);
- it simultaneously kills x’s most dangerous potential four (the diagonal (2,2)-(3,3)-(4,4)-(5,5));
- it forces x onto the low-value square (1,2);
- it leaves the original diagonal three intact as a reserved threat that can be activated later (x can never safely play column 1, because doing so would make (2,1) droppable for o).

All other legal columns create no threat, surrender the initiative, and allow x to occupy (5,5) or (2,2) and seize a forcing sequence. Consequently the unique optimal action is column 2.

Final Answer: 2

